From f0f113de13698d926a3a561ec89855f35de976ea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Helmut Januschka <helmut@januschka.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 17:43:39 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 05/11] hires mic: decode the uplink and publish a PipeWire
 source

Decodes each AAC-ELD access unit through the system libavcodec and
writes s16 into a FIFO that module-pipe-source publishes as
AirPodsHiRes at 64 kHz mono. The decoder needs the uplink's 48 kHz
coding rate and its four-byte AudioSpecificConfig; the stream itself
decodes to 64 kHz.

Measured on AirPods Max 2 with this daemon holding the control link:
400 access units, 400 frames decoded, no errors, and a 9.9 s capture
from the published source at 64 kHz mono carrying speech. The card
stayed where ear detection had left it throughout, since the uplink
rides AACP rather than an audio profile.

The fifo is opened read-write so the writer never blocks on a missing
reader, and a full pipe drops the frame rather than stalling the
control link.

Not ported from the reference implementation: its automatic gain
control. Captures here measure quieter than the Rust daemon's, which is
that difference and not a decode fault.
